\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{One coordinate reflection is reflection positive}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{On the nearest-neighbor torus of \ref{fdip-007M}, with every
weight positive and invariant under #{\theta}, and with mass
#{m>0}, the massive scalar law of \ref{fdip-007O} is reflection
positive along #{\theta}. This holds for a
\vocabk{site reflection}{fdip-007M} and for a
\vocabk{link reflection}{fdip-007M}.
The same conclusion holds on the symmetric torus of
\ref{fdip-00PY}, whose underlying undirected graph and weights
are those of \ref{fdip-007O}.}

\proof{
\p{The undirected nearest-neighbor graph, the nonnegative
weights, and the mass are the data of \ref{fdip-007O}. That
theorem gives reflection invariance and reflection positivity
for either coordinate reflection. Adding the opposite
orientation of each edge does not change the scalar energy,
so the law on the symmetric torus is the same law.}
}
